<p>Workcell Studio remains a source prerelease, verified with Kujo 1.6.0 and Workcell 1.3.0. Its real Chrome WebMCP failure/repair/Eval checks do not certify a public deployment.</p>
<p>Foreman, SSG WebMCP, and VideoOps are source releases. Their release notes define installation and verification scope; publication does not certify a hosted service, cloud provider, or media entitlement.</p>
<p>The Kujo Pi row was refreshed on September 26, 2026. Other entries retain their individually documented inventory dates.</p>
<h2 id="recent-changes-worth-checking">Recent changes worth checking</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Kujo Pi 1.1.0:</strong> approval-gated Ability tools, opt-in Watchdog v2 metadata, bounded telemetry and artifact inspection, and receipt-failure recovery. Read <a href="/tools/kujo-pi/">Kujo Pi</a>.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><strong>AI Chat 1.3.0:</strong> live code review, durable supervision, isolated worktrees, scoped MCP connections, an attention inbox, a command palette, and persistent chat tabs. Follow its Node, storage, permission, and worktree upgrade requirements; the historical eight-hour soak remains incomplete. Read <a href="/showcases/ai-chat/">AI Chat</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Workcell 1.3.0:</strong> Docker/Podman execution and failure preservation, with Git assurance/process correlation experimental and remote adapters still alpha. Use the package&apos;s qualified Kujo 1.6.0 runtime. Read <a href="/tools/workcell/">Workcell</a>.</li>
<li><strong>SiteProbe 0.3.0:</strong> native Kujo product commands and platform-qualified source archives; its test tooling can still require Python. Read <a href="/tools/siteprobe/">SiteProbe</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Skills 0.7.1, Workflows 0.6.2, Agents 1.4.0:</strong> 135 skills, 44 local workflows, and canonical VideoOps tools and schemas. Live media permission and provider limits remain explicit. Read <a href="/collections/skills/">skills</a> and <a href="/collections/workflows/">workflows</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Commerce 0.5.1:</strong> adds optional PostgreSQL processing, Square payments/refunds, embedded checkout, billing workflows, OAuth connections, and recovery. Static hosted links remain lightweight. Install the versioned npm package; advanced features retain their acceptance gates. Read <a href="/tools/commerce/">Commerce</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Scout 1.1.0:</strong> bounded rooted reads, aggregate limits, security exports, deterministic performance and labeled-corpus gates, stable CI diagnostics, and native Windows contract coverage. It requires Kujo 1.5.0 or newer. Read <a href="/tools/scout/">Scout</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Watchdog 1.1.0:</strong> canonical v2 telemetry, JSONL/OTLP projections, Connected Sources management, and named proxy-profile changes that apply to new requests without restart. Read <a href="/tools/watchdog/">Watchdog</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Agents SDK 1.1.2 and RunLedger 1.2.0:</strong> released lifecycle telemetry and evidence-correlation work accompanies Kujo 1.6. RAG and Eval retain separately documented source work beyond their published releases.</li>
<li><strong>Dispatch 1.3.0:</strong> durable review, policy-preserving resume and restart-safe control with Kujo 1.6.0. Wave C beta and Wave D alpha remain experimental; a separate selected-effect operator API does not authorize parent replay. Read <a href="/tools/dispatch/">Dispatch</a>.</li>
<li><strong>CaseFile, Fence, Scent, ShipCheck, Howl, Redact, and Dossier:</strong> recent source hardening has its own verification evidence. Pin the reviewed source when adopting those fixes; an unchanged release archive does not acquire later commits.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="tags-and-source-only-packages">Tags and source-only packages</h2>
<p>The <a href="/ecosystem/providers/">25-provider index</a> records pinned source tags and driver compatibility. Many provider tags have no corresponding GitHub Release. Their tag-based installation instructions remain valid; do not substitute an older GitHub Release solely because it has a release page. AI SDK&apos;s documented 1.1.0 provider baseline is likewise distinct from its latest GitHub Release.</p>
<p>Kujo Pi 1.1.0 is published on GitHub and npm; install <code>npm:@kujolang/kujo-pi@1.1.0</code> through Pi. See <a href="/tools/kujo-pi/">Kujo Pi</a> for its separate host and runtime requirements. Repositories without a GitHub Release, including infrastructure and some application sources, are not assigned invented release versions here. Private projects retain their documented access boundaries.</p>
<h2 id="agent-discovery">Agent discovery</h2>
<p>The public <a href="https://mcp.kujolang.ai/mcp">Kujo MCP catalog</a> exposes read-only project, skill, workflow, and installer guidance. It is separate from the MCP framework and from private application gateways. Refreshes publish a reviewed static snapshot; they do not execute installer commands or grant application permissions.</p>
<h2 id="kujo-1-8-runtime-and-companion-delivery">Kujo 1.8 runtime and companion delivery</h2>
<p>Kujo 1.8 does not republish or silently upgrade companion tools. RunLedger 1.2.0, AI SDK 1.2.0 and Agents SDK 1.1.2 are published independently. AI SDK 1.2.0 adds state persistence, strict accounting, and transport controls; its release notes distinguish offline verification from live-provider evidence. Workcell, Ability and MCP 1.2.0 distribute the controlled-execution cohort while retaining experimental assurance/interoperability boundaries. Dispatch 1.3.0 completes this controller release cohort with the same experimental boundaries. Stop older workers and follow its backup/upgrade instructions before changing persisted-state versions.</p>
<p>SSG 1.2.0 requires pinned esbuild 0.28.2 for minified builds and adds explicit private-image opt-in and a separate DocGen repository-CI budget. See the <a href="/showcases/ssg/#upgrade-and-integration-notes">SSG upgrade notes</a>.</p>
<h2 id="agents-sdk-1-1-3">Agents SDK 1.1.3</h2>
<p>The October 13 patch hardens approval, handoff, state, storage, retry, and token-accounting behavior while preserving public contracts. It supersedes 1.1.2 for new installations. See the <a href="/tools/agents-sdk/">Agents SDK guide</a> for compatibility notes and the remaining cost-unit conversion limit.</p>
<h2 id="october-14-capability-updates">October 14 capability updates</h2>
<p>PatchBrief 1.0.2, CaseFile 1.0.1, MCP 1.3.2, ShipCheck 1.0.1, Scent 1.1.0, Kennel 1.2.0, Fence 1.1.0, Concord 1.1.1, Eval 1.1.0, CRUD API 1.0.1 and Dispatch 1.4.0 are published. Dispatch 1.4.0 requires Kujo 1.8.1. RAG 1.1.0 remains an unpublished candidate pending required review evidence; local functional tests do not certify hosted deployments.</p>
<h2 id="kujo-opencode-launch-2026-10-18">Kujo OpenCode launch — 2026-10-18</h2>
<p><a href="/tools/kujo-opencode/">Kujo OpenCode 0.2.0</a> is published on npm and GitHub. It brings six specialists, 62 supported capabilities, native approvals and inspectable receipts to OpenCode 2. Registry installation and upgrade checks passed. Native host checks cover Linux, macOS and Windows; live specialist evidence uses macOS and ChatGPT. Install through npm, not Kennel.</p>
<h2 id="kujo-1-8-2-maintenance-release">Kujo 1.8.2 maintenance release</h2>
<p><a href="https://github.com/kujolang/kujo/releases/tag/v1.8.2">Kujo 1.8.2</a> resolves ZIP security advisories and retired YAML, credential-store and bincode dependencies. It preserves Kujo archive behavior, stored credentials and legacy hyphenation dictionaries, corrects native dictionary handling and tightens capability and filesystem boundaries. Bounded HTTP stream collection is available. Rust embedders accessing <code>Value::ZipArchive.writer</code> must follow the <a href="https://github.com/kujolang/kujo/blob/v1.8.2/docs/ZIP_9_RUST_MIGRATION.md">ZIP 9 migration guide</a>; Kujo scripts require no archive API migration.</p>
<p>See the <a href="https://github.com/kujolang/kujo/blob/main/docs/RELEASE_NOTES_1_8_2.md">release notes</a>. Rust 1.89 remains the source-build minimum; experimental companion protocols and participant SDKs are not promoted by this patch.</p>
<h2 id="kujo-1-9-0-runtime-release">Kujo 1.9.0 runtime release</h2>
<p><a href="https://github.com/kujolang/kujo/releases/tag/v1.9.0">Kujo 1.9.0</a> adds checked nested collection and struct writes, strict UTF-8 conversion, byte iteration, fallible comparator sorting, regex callbacks and match counting, headerless CSV, decimal formatting and wrapping arithmetic. Shared bytecode and copy-on-write storage improvements preserve retained values and atomic failed writes.</p>
<p>Native installation and upgrades from 1.8.2 and npm installation passed on all five targets. The <a href="https://github.com/kujolang/kujo/blob/main/release/kujo-1.9.0-publication.json">publication record</a> identifies the exact source and verification runs. Companion protocols and participant SDKs retain their experimental boundaries.</p>
<h2 id="kujo-hermes-0-1-0">Kujo Hermes 0.1.0</h2>
<p><a href="/tools/kujo-hermes/">Kujo Hermes</a> adds native engineering checks, workspace trust, human approvals and execution receipts to Hermes. Install through Hermes; Kujo itself is a separate prerequisite. <a href="https://github.com/kujolang/kujo-hermes/releases/tag/v0.1.0">Release and evidence</a>.</p></div>
